\title{Local Checkability and Multi-Round Soundness Amplification for Zero-Knowledge Certification}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-02}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We isolate and prove the soundness core of zero-knowledge certification protocols in a form entirely independent of the internal structure of the verifier's check. A \emph{locally checkable certificate} over a finite challenge space $\Omega$ is a Boolean predicate assigning to each challenge a pass/fail verdict; it is \emph{globally valid} when every challenge passes. Our first result, \textbf{Single-Round Soundness}, shows that if a certificate is invalid---some challenge fails---then the set of passing challenges has at most $|\Omega|-1$ elements, so a verifier sampling a uniformly random challenge rejects with probability at least $1/|\Omega|$; this holds with no assumption on the checker. Our main result, \textbf{Multi-Round Soundness Amplification}, lifts this to $k$ independent rounds: a prover forced to commit to an invalid certificate each round survives all rounds with probability at most $\big((|\Omega|-1)/|\Omega|\big)^k$, which decays geometrically to $0$. We prove a companion \textbf{Strict Soundness Gap}, and finally instantiate the machinery at the classical Goldreich--Micali--Wigderson zero-knowledge protocol for graph $3$-colourability, obtaining the fully amplified bound $\big((|E|-1)/|E|\big)^k$ for a prover committing to improper colourings. This upgrades the classical single-round gap to a statement with vanishing error and exposes the tight round complexity $\Theta(|\Omega|\cdot k)$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

A zero-knowledge proof convinces a verifier that a statement is true while revealing nothing beyond its truth. Such protocols are foundational to cryptography and, increasingly, to the certification of mathematical and computational claims: one can certify that a theorem has a valid proof, or that a computation was performed correctly, without disclosing the proof or computation. The intuition behind many such systems is that of \emph{probabilistically checkable proofs} (PCPs): a claimed proof can be re-encoded so that a small number of random spot-checks detects any flaw with constant probability, and repetition amplifies confidence to certainty.

This paper distills the \emph{soundness} half of that intuition to its logical minimum. We separate two concerns:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Local checkability}---the structural property that an invalid certificate must fail at least one local check. We treat this as a hypothesis and prove exactly what soundness it buys, with no assumption on the checker's internals.
  \item \textbf{Amplification}---the probabilistic consequence of many independent rounds. We prove the exact geometric decay and identify the tight base of the exponential.
\end{itemize}
Specializing to the Goldreich--Micali--Wigderson (GMW) protocol for graph $3$-colourability yields a new statement: whereas the classical analysis gives only a single-round gap of $1/|E|$, we obtain a full $k$-round bound $\big((|E|-1)/|E|\big)^k$ with vanishing error.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $\beta$ is a type with decidable equality and $\Omega$ is a finite \textbf{challenge space} with cardinality $|\Omega|$.

\begin{definition}[Locally checkable certificate]
A \emph{locally checkable certificate} over $\Omega$ is a function $\chk : \beta \to \{\texttt{true},\texttt{false}\}$. A challenge $e \in \Omega$ \emph{passes} if $\chk(e)=\texttt{true}$ and \emph{fails} otherwise. The \textbf{passing set} is
\[
  P(\chk) = \{\, e \in \Omega : \chk(e) = \texttt{true} \,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Validity]
The certificate is \textbf{globally valid} if $P(\chk)=\Omega$, and \textbf{invalid} if some challenge fails: $\exists\, e \in \Omega,\ \chk(e)=\texttt{false}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Verifier]
The \emph{single-round verifier} samples $e \in \Omega$ uniformly and accepts iff $e$ passes; its accepting probability is $|P(\chk)|/|\Omega|$. The \emph{$k$-round verifier} runs $k$ independent single rounds with certificates $\chk_1,\dots,\chk_k$ and accepts iff all sampled challenges pass, with accepting probability $\prod_{i=1}^k |P(\chk_i)|/|\Omega|$.
\end{definition}

\section{Single-round soundness}

\begin{theorem}[Single-Round Soundness]\label{thm:single}
Let $\chk$ be a certificate over $\Omega$ and suppose some $e \in \Omega$ satisfies $\chk(e)=\texttt{false}$. Then
\[
  |P(\chk)| \le |\Omega| - 1,
\]
equivalently the single-round verifier rejects with probability at least $1/|\Omega|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix a failing challenge $e \in \Omega$. We claim $P(\chk) \subseteq \Omega \setminus \{e\}$. Take $x \in P(\chk)$: then $x \in \Omega$ and $\chk(x)=\texttt{true}$. If $x=e$ then $\chk(e)=\texttt{true}$, contradicting $\chk(e)=\texttt{false}$; hence $x \ne e$ and $x \in \Omega \setminus \{e\}$. Monotonicity of cardinality gives $|P(\chk)| \le |\Omega \setminus \{e\}| = |\Omega| - 1$, the last step because $e \in \Omega$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The proof uses no property of $\chk$ beyond a single failing point. This is the precise sense in which local checkability alone yields a soundness gap of at least $1/|\Omega|$.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Strict Soundness Gap]\label{thm:strict}
If $|\Omega|>0$ and the certificate is invalid, then $|P(\chk)|/|\Omega| < 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $|\Omega|>0$, dividing by it preserves inequalities, so it suffices to show $|P(\chk)|<|\Omega|$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:single}, $|P(\chk)| \le |\Omega|-1$ as naturals; casting to $\mathbb{Q}$ (valid since $|\Omega|\ge 1$) gives $|P(\chk)| \le |\Omega|-1 < |\Omega|$. Divide by $|\Omega|>0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Multi-round soundness amplification}

\begin{theorem}[Multi-Round Soundness Amplification]\label{thm:multi}
Let $|\Omega|>0$ and let $\chk_1,\dots,\chk_k$ be certificates over $\Omega$, each invalid. Then
\[
  \prod_{i=1}^{k} \frac{|P(\chk_i)|}{|\Omega|} \;\le\; \left( \frac{|\Omega|-1}{|\Omega|} \right)^{k}.
\]
Since $0 \le (|\Omega|-1)/|\Omega| < 1$, the bound decays geometrically to $0$ as $k \to \infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Work in $\mathbb{Q}$. Each factor $|P(\chk_i)|/|\Omega|$ is nonnegative. By Theorem~\ref{thm:single}, $|P(\chk_i)| \le |\Omega|-1$ as naturals; casting gives $|P(\chk_i)|/|\Omega| \le (|\Omega|-1)/|\Omega| =: r$. All factors are nonnegative and bounded by the constant $r$, so the monotone product inequality yields $\prod_{i=1}^k |P(\chk_i)|/|\Omega| \le \prod_{i=1}^k r = r^k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Error target]\label{cor:rounds}
To force the survival probability below $2^{-k}$ it suffices to run $R$ rounds with $r^R \le 2^{-k}$, i.e. $R \ge \dfrac{k\ln 2}{\ln(|\Omega|/(|\Omega|-1))}$. Since $\ln(|\Omega|/(|\Omega|-1)) \approx 1/|\Omega|$ for large $|\Omega|$, this is $R=\Theta(|\Omega|\cdot k)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Instantiation: zero-knowledge proof of graph 3-colourability}

Let $V$ be a finite vertex type with decidable equality and $E$ a finite edge set of ordered pairs. A \textbf{$3$-colouring} is a map $c : V \to \{0,1,2\}$.

\begin{definition}[Proper colouring]
$c$ is \emph{proper} for $E$ if $c(e_1) \ne c(e_2)$ for every $e \in E$, and \emph{improper} otherwise.
\end{definition}

\paragraph{The GMW protocol (one round).} The prover holds a proper colouring $c$, samples a uniformly random permutation $\pi$ of $\{0,1,2\}$, and commits to $\pi \circ c$. The verifier challenges a uniformly random edge $(u,v)\in E$; the prover opens $(\pi(c(u)),\pi(c(v)))$; the verifier accepts iff they differ.

\emph{Completeness:} permutations are injective, so a proper colouring stays proper under $\pi$, and the honest prover always opens distinct colours. \emph{Perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge:} for a fixed edge with distinct endpoint colours $a\ne b$, the map $\pi \mapsto (\pi(a),\pi(b))$ is a bijection from the six permutations of $\{0,1,2\}$ onto the six ordered distinct pairs, so the opened view is uniform over distinct pairs, independent of $c$.

To connect to the abstract theory, set $\Omega := E$ and define, for a colouring $c$, the check $\chk_c(e) := [\, c(e_1)\ne c(e_2)\,]$. The passing set $P(\chk_c)$ is the set of edges with distinct endpoint colours, and $\chk_c$ is invalid precisely when $c$ is improper.

\begin{theorem}[Three-Colouring Amplified Soundness]\label{thm:g3c}
Suppose $|E|>0$. Let $c_1,\dots,c_k$ be colourings, each improper for $E$. Then
\[
  \prod_{i=1}^{k} \frac{|\{ e \in E : c_i(e_1) \ne c_i(e_2)\}|}{|E|} \;\le\; \left( \frac{|E|-1}{|E|} \right)^{k},
\]
which decays geometrically to $0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For each $i$, improperness gives an edge $e\in E$ with $c_i(e_1)=c_i(e_2)$, so $\chk_{c_i}(e)=\texttt{false}$ and $\chk_{c_i}$ is invalid over $\Omega=E$. Its passing set is $\{e\in E : c_i(e_1)\ne c_i(e_2)\}$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:multi} with $\Omega=E$ and $\chk_i=\chk_{c_i}$; the base is $(|E|-1)/|E|$.
\end{proof}

The classical single-round gap is the $k=1$ shadow of Theorem~\ref{thm:g3c}; the novelty is recognizing it as an instance of the assumption-free principle of Theorem~\ref{thm:single} and multiplying across independent rounds via Theorem~\ref{thm:multi}.

\section{Tight round complexity}

\begin{proposition}[Tightness]\label{prop:tight}
The bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:multi} is achieved. For $|\Omega|=n\ge 1$, a prover corrupting exactly one location per round has per-round accepting fraction exactly $(n-1)/n$ and $k$-round survival probability exactly $((n-1)/n)^k$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A single-failure certificate has passing set of size $n-1$, so its accepting fraction is $(n-1)/n$, meeting Theorem~\ref{thm:single} with equality; independence multiplies to $((n-1)/n)^k$.
\end{proof}

Thus reaching error $2^{-k}$ by independent uniform single-location challenges requires $\Theta(n\cdot k)$ rounds, and no such schedule beats $((n-1)/n)^{\text{rounds}}$. The folklore ``$O(k)$ rounds'' holds only when the per-round gap is constant---i.e.\ when each query inspects a constant fraction of the certificate, which is precisely what PCP-style re-encodings supply.

\section{Discussion and future work}

Concrete zero-knowledge protocols differ in commitment machinery, arithmetization, and simulators, yet their soundness typically reduces to the same skeleton: a uniform random local check with a guaranteed failing location. Proving that skeleton once, with no hypothesis on the checker, gives a reusable template, as the GMW specialization shows. Combined with a binding hash-tree commitment---which fixes the certificate before challenges are drawn and enforces the ``invalid each round'' hypothesis---the amplified soundness here forms the backbone of certify-without-revealing protocols, with applications from sealed-bid proof-strategy auctions to succinct blockchain validity proofs.

\paragraph{Future directions.} (i) \emph{Binding is free; only uniqueness costs security}: tree commitments from any two-argument compression function are binding in a constructive, assumption-free sense, with collision resistance needed only for uniqueness. (ii) \emph{Tight round complexity} $\Theta(n\cdot k)$, not $O(k)$, is optimal for independent single-location challenges. (iii) \emph{Constant soundness gap via correlated queries}: a $\mathrm{poly}(n)$-size re-encoding should yield a local checker with per-round gap $\ge 1/2$, reaching error $2^{-k}$ in $O(k)$ rounds independent of $n$---the algorithmic heart of the PCP theorem in local-checkability language.

\section{Conclusion}

Single-Round Soundness caps the accepting fraction of an invalid certificate at $(|\Omega|-1)/|\Omega|$ with no assumption on the checker; Multi-Round Soundness Amplification multiplies this to $\big((|\Omega|-1)/|\Omega|\big)^k$, which is tight and, specialized to graph $3$-colourability, gives $\big((|E|-1)/|E|\big)^k$. The tightness sharpens the folklore round count to $\Theta(|\Omega|\cdot k)$ and locates exactly where PCP-style re-encodings must intervene.

